### Setup

Work modulo $p$. Granite chooses $c \in \{2,\dots,p-1\}$, so $c \not\equiv 0,1 \pmod p$. Pomegranate then chooses $d,x$ and sets $f(t)=ct+d$. The paper records the orbit
$$a_0=x,\quad a_1=f(x),\quad a_2=f^2(x),\quad \dots$$
with $a_n$ written by Pomegranate when $n$ is even and by Granite when $n$ is odd. The game ends at the first $n\ge 1$ with $a_n\equiv a_m\pmod p$ for some $m<n$; the writer of $a_n$ wins.

### Structure of the orbit

Since $c\not\equiv 0$, $f$ is a permutation of $\mathbb F_p$. Since $c\not\equiv 1$, $f$ has a unique fixed point $t^\star = d/(1-c)$. Conjugating by the translation $u=t-t^\star$ turns $f$ into multiplication by $c$:
$$u_n = c^n u_0, \qquad u_0 = x - t^\star.$$

- If $u_0=0$ (i.e. $x=t^\star$), the orbit is constant: $a_1\equiv a_0$, first repeat at $n=1$ (odd) $\Rightarrow$ **Granite wins**.
- If $u_0\ne 0$, the orbit is a single cycle of length $L=\operatorname{ord}_p(c)$: the values $a_0,\dots,a_{L-1}$ are distinct and $a_L\equiv a_0$. The first repeat is at $n=L$, so the winner is Pomegranate iff $L$ is even, and Granite iff $L$ is odd.

### Reducing to a condition on $c$

For a fixed $c$, Pomegranate (moving second, choosing $d,x$) wins iff she can force an even $L$. The only choice affecting $L$ is whether $x=t^\star$:

- $x=t^\star$: $L=1$ (odd) — Granite wins.
- $x\ne t^\star$: $L=\operatorname{ord}_p(c)$.

So Pomegranate wins against $c$ iff $\operatorname{ord}_p(c)$ is even (then pick any $x\ne t^\star$, always possible since $p\ge 3$). If $\operatorname{ord}_p(c)$ is odd, Granite wins against every choice of $d,x$.

Granite picks $c$ to make Pomegranate lose. Hence:

> **Pomegranate has a winning strategy** $\iff$ every $c\in\{2,\dots,p-1\}$ has even multiplicative order mod $p$.

### Characterizing the primes

$\mathbb F_p^\times$ is cyclic of order $p-1$. Write $p-1=2^s\cdot m$ with $m$ odd. The elements of odd order form the unique subgroup $H\le \mathbb F_p^\times$ of order $m$.

The condition "every $c\in\{2,\dots,p-1\}$ has even order" means no element of $\{2,\dots,p-1\}$ lies in $H$. Since $H\subseteq\mathbb F_p^\times=\{1,2,\dots,p-1\}$, this is equivalent to $H\subseteq\{1\}$, i.e. $|H|=m=1$, i.e.
$$p-1 = 2^s.$$

Conversely, if $p-1=2^s$, every element of $\mathbb F_p^\times$ has order a power of $2$; any $c\ne 1$ (and $c\in\{2,\dots,p-1\}$ excludes $c=1$) has order $\ge 2$, hence even. So the condition is exactly $p-1$ is a power of $2$.

### Listing the primes

$p=2^s+1$ prime with $p$ odd and $p<10^5$. For $2^s+1$ to be prime, $s$ must itself be a power of $2$ (if $s$ has an odd divisor $r>1$, then $2^{s/r}+1\mid 2^s+1$). Thus $s\in\{1,2,4,8,16,32,\dots\}$, giving
$$p\in\{3,\ 5,\ 17,\ 257,\ 65537,\ 2^{32}+1,\dots\}.$$
The bound $p<10^5=100000$ excludes $2^{32}+1\approx 4.3\times10^9$ and beyond. The remaining Fermat primes are
$$3,\ 5,\ 17,\ 257,\ 65537.$$
(Each is indeed prime: $3,5,17,257$ are classical; $65537$ is the largest known Fermat prime.)

### Sum

$$3+5+17+257+65537 = 65819.$$

